  "intro": {
    "text": "Python has two everyday kinds of number: whole numbers like `7` (**int**) and numbers with a decimal point like `2.5` (**float**).\n\nThe math signs are `+`, `-`, `*` (times) and `/` (divide). `/` always gives a float, so `10 / 2` is `5.0`.\n\nTwo more come in pairs: `//` divides and drops the leftover fraction (`17 // 5` is `3`), and `%` gives only the leftover, the **remainder** (`17 % 5` is `2`). `**` means \"to the power of\": `3 ** 2` is `9`.\n\nPython works out `**` first, then `* / // %`, then `+ -`, going left to right. Anything in brackets goes first. Turn on the **working it out** panel to watch it happen, one step at a time.",
    "analogy": "Sharing 17 sweets between 5 friends: each friend gets `17 // 5` sweets (3) and `17 % 5` sweets are left in the bag (2). Floor division is the fair share, the remainder is what is left over."
  },
